<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="Aspire.Hosting.Testing" />
		<PackageReference Include="AwesomeAssertions" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.Testing" />
		<PackageReference Include="NSwag.MSBuild" />
		<PackageReference Include="Respawn" />
		<PackageReference Include="TUnit" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Api\Api.csproj" />
		<ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Aspire\AppHost\AppHost.csproj" />
		<ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Infrastructure\Infrastructure.csproj" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!--
	Generates ApiClient.cs, the typed client these tests drive the API with.

	It lives here rather than in Api.csproj, where it used to, because the
	client is this project's artifact: a production project whose build wrote
	into ../../tests/ was an inversion that ArchitectureTests cannot see - it
	enforces Clean Architecture on code references, and nothing enforces
	anything on the build graph.

	BeforeCompile, not AfterBuild: the generated file has to exist before this
	project compiles. The ProjectReference on Api.csproj is what makes that
	safe - Api builds first, and its own GenerateOpenApiDocuments target
	refreshes wwwroot/openapi-v1.json, so NSwag always reads a current
	document. Nothing here has to sequence that by hand.

	No Configuration condition. The document is regenerated in every
	configuration (Api.csproj sets OpenApiGenerateDocumentsOnBuild
	unconditionally), so a Debug-only client meant `dotnet build -c Release`
	rewrote one committed artifact and left the other stale, silently. The
	release path does not pay for this: src/Api/Dockerfile builds Api.csproj
	alone and never compiles this project.

	A plain Exec, deliberately. This used to be ContinueOnError plus a captured
	ConsoleOutput and a hand-rolled <Error>, which could never work: capturing
	needs ConsoleToMSBuild="true" and the attribute was absent, so a failing
	NSwag aborted the build with the text "(No message specified)". It was
	broken for its whole life without anyone noticing, which says what the
	cleverness was worth. Exec echoes NSwag's own output to the build log and
	fails with it.
	-->
	<Target Name="GenerateApiClient" BeforeTargets="BeforeCompile">
		<Exec
			WorkingDirectory="$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)"
			Command="$(NSwagExe_Net100) run nswag.json /variables:ApiVersion=v1"
		/>
	</Target>

</Project>
